{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786510219,"nonce":"6Aq2KwBEXvK4rNBxgZuLvXNQL7k=","title":"Title: Group ack, snooze, unsnooze and comment fan out one full write transaction per member with no ceiling","message":"**What is wrong.** `Service.fanOut` (`internal/grouping/service/read.go:355-395`) loads every currently-joined member and applies the verb once per distinct alert, sequentially, inside whatever called it. The member list comes from `CurrentMemberAlerts` → `memberAlertsSQL`: `WHERE m.org_id = $1 AND m.group_id = $2 AND m.left_at IS NULL ORDER BY m.joined_at ASC`, with no `LIMIT` (`internal/grouping/repository/member.go:346-350`). The loop at `read.go:369-393` has no chunking and no per-request ceiling.\n\nFour verbs go through it: `Acknowledge` (`read.go:269-270`), `Comment` (`:301-302`), `Snooze` (`:331-332`) and `Unsnooze` (`:342-343`).\n\n**The demonstration.** Each iteration is not one statement. `AcknowledgeAs` (`internal/alerts/service/seam.go:120-130`) calls `Service.Acknowledge` → `setAck`, which opens its own transaction (`internal/alerts/service/actions.go:70`) and inside it reads the alert (`:71`), reads the open occurrence (`:75`), writes `SetAck` with an optimistic version check (`:127`), writes `SetProjection` (`:133`), and appends the timeline event — itself a dedupe-key claim plus an insert (`internal/alerts/repository/event.go:117-133`). That is six statements and one commit, per member, in series.\n\nAt the storm figure the grouping code names for itself — \"a storm of five thousand\" (`internal/grouping/repository/member.go:294`) — one press of the group Acknowledge button is ~5 000 sequential write transactions and ~30 000 statements inside one HTTP request. Nothing bounds it, and `fanOut` does not return early: a member that refuses the verb is counted and skipped (`read.go:376-389`), so the loop always runs to the end of the list.\n\nThe Slack path reaches the same loop under a job timeout instead of a client. `slackGroupActions.AcknowledgeGroup` calls `g.grouping.Acknowledge` at `internal/app/adapters.go:1219`; the interaction runs on a queue whose registered timeouts in this range are 30 seconds to 2 minutes (`internal/platform/jobs/registry.go:179-215`). A fan-out that outlives the timeout is retried, and the retry re-walks the whole membership from the start.\n\n**Why it matters.** The rest of oto is explicit that unbounded sweeps are the failure mode to design out: `internal/app/workers.go:140-142`, on `sweepLimit = 500` — *\"A sweep that is not bounded is a sweep that becomes an outage the first time somebody has a bad night.\"* A group ack during a storm is that night. `fanOut`'s own comment is about the right thing (`read.go:350-354`: not failing all 39 members because one resolved) and silent about how many members there might be.\n\n`FanOutResult` already reports `Members`, `Applied` and `SkippedCodes` (`read.go:367`, `:384-392`), so a partial fan-out is already an expressible answer — the shape needed to bound this exists.\n\n**Where.** `internal/grouping/service/read.go:355-395` (`fanOut`), `:269-270`, `:301-302`, `:331-332`, `:342-343` (the four verbs), `internal/grouping/repository/member.go:346-350` (`memberAlertsSQL`, no `LIMIT`), `internal/grouping/api/handlers.go:99,188,235` (the HTTP callers), `internal/app/adapters.go:1219` (the Slack caller), `internal/alerts/service/actions.go:60-142` (the per-member transaction).\n\n**Scope.** medium\n\n**Done when.** `fanOut` applies a per-call ceiling and reports what it did not reach through the existing `FanOutResult`, or each verb becomes set-based over the membership; the Slack interaction path completes inside its registered timeout at five thousand members.","files":null}]}